1. What Is a Linked List?
          An array stores elements next to each other conceptually:

          Array:          
                    index
                     0    1    2    3
                     ↓    ↓    ↓    ↓
                    [10] [20] [30] [40]

          A linked list is different.
          
          Each node contains:
                    value + reference to next node

          For example: 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → null

          Conceptually:
          +------+------+
          |  10  |  ●───┼──────┐
          +------+------+
                              ↓
                        +------+------+
                        |  20  |  ●───┼──────┐
                        +------+------+
                                            ↓
                                      +------+------+
                                      |  30  |  ●───┼──────┐
                                      +------+------+
                                                          ↓
                                                    +------+------+
                                                    |  40  | null |
                                                    +------+------+

The important thing is:  A node doesn't know where the entire list is. It only knows where the next node is.
================================================================================================================================================
2. Java Node Definition
          For interview problems, you'll commonly see:
                    
                    class ListNode {
                        int val;
                        ListNode next;
                    
                        ListNode() {}
                    
                        ListNode(int val) {
                            this.val = val;
                        }
                    
                        ListNode(int val, ListNode next) {
                            this.val = val;
                            this.next = next;
                        }
                    }

A list:   1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null

can be created as:
                    ListNode head = new ListNode(1);
                    
                    head.next = new ListNode(2);
                    head.next.next = new ListNode(3);
                    head.next.next.next = new ListNode(4);

But in interviews, you generally don't need to construct the input manually.

The platform gives you:     ListNode head

and you manipulate it.

================================================================================================================================================
3. The Most Important Linked-List Concept

          This:    head
                     is not the linked list itself.          
                    It is a reference to the first node.

          Think:
          head
           ↓
          1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null

          If you lose head without another reference, you can lose access to the entire list.

          This is why pointer manipulation needs to be deliberate.

