4. Basic Traversal

                    Suppose: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null
                    
                    To traverse:                    
                              ListNode current = head;                    
                              while (current != null) {                              
                                  System.out.println(current.val);                              
                                  current = current.next;
                              }
                    
                    The critical line is:  current = current.next;
                    
                    It means:   Move the pointer to the next node.




================================================================================================================================================
5. Traversal Invariant

          This is where we start thinking at L5 level.
          
          At the beginning of every iteration:          
                    current points to the next node that still needs to be processed.
          
          For example:          
                    1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null
                    ↑
                    current
          
          After one iteration:          
                    1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null
                        ↑
                      current
          
          After another:          
                    1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → null
                            ↑
                          current
          
          This is an invariant.          
          An invariant is simply:          
                    A property that remains true at a particular point in every iteration.
          
          This concept becomes extremely powerful with linked lists.




================================================================================================================================================
6. How Linked Lists is Different From Arrays

          Array problems usually involve:          
                                        index

          For example:          
                    nums[i]
                    nums[i + 1]
                    nums[i - 1]

          Linked lists don't have direct indexing.

          To get the 100th node, you generally have to traverse:
          
          1 → 2 → 3 → ... → 100

          Therefore:

          Access by index:
                    Array       → O(1)
                    Linked List → O(n)
================================================================================================================================================
7. Linked List Complexity

          | Operation                       | Singly Linked List |
          | ------------------------------- | -----------------: |
          | Access by index                 |               O(n) |
          | Search                          |               O(n) |
          | Insert at head                  |               O(1) |
          | Delete head                     |               O(1) |
          | Insert after known node         |               O(1) |
          | Delete after known node         |               O(1) |
          | Insert at end with tail pointer |               O(1) |
          | Insert at end without tail      |               O(n) |


This is why linked lists are useful when we need frequent pointer-based insertions/deletions.

